/*
===============================================================================
SystemVerilog Program Blocks
===============================================================================

Concept
-------
A 'program' block is a special testbench container that executes in the simulator's **reactive** region, i.e., **after** the DUT (modules) have updated.
This ordering avoids TB↔DUT races.


Why Use It
----------
• Avoid race conditions between TB and DUT without manual #delays.
• Ensure TB samples DUT outputs after they settle, and schedules inputs for the
  next cycle.


Event-Queue Position (simplified)
---------------------------------
Modules (DUT) update  →  Program (TB) reacts  →  Postponed/monitoring
(DUT first; program later → deterministic TB sampling/driving)


What It Contains
----------------
• Non-synthesizable TB code (initial/final, tasks/functions, checks/prints).
• Typically used with an interface to access DUT pins.


Common Today?
-------------
• Educational/simple benches: Yes, still seen.
• Professional/UVM testbenches: Rare. UVM phases + interfaces + clocking blocks
  provide the same race-free behavior with richer structure.


Mini Example
------------
program automatic tb(my_if IF);
  initial begin
    IF.req <= 0;
    @(posedge IF.clk);
    IF.req <= 1;     // driven safely after DUT updates
    @(posedge IF.clk);
    $display("q=%0b", IF.q); // stable sample
    $finish;
  end
endprogram

Interview Pointers
------------------
• Purpose: "Separate TB from DUT in the scheduler to avoid races."
• Runs After: "DUT (modules) — program executes in the reactive region."
• In UVM: "We generally use UVM phases + clocking blocks; program blocks are
  optional and uncommon in modern flows."

Takeaways
---------
• Program blocks solve the **ordering** problem (race avoidance).
• In modern DV, prefer **interfaces + clocking blocks + UVM phases**.


When should you use it?
If you’re writing tiny, standalone benches, a program is okay.
If you’re aiming for industry-ready style (UVM), skip program blocks and use:
Interfaces to bundle pins, Clocking blocks to define safe drive/sample timing,
UVM for execution order (phases) and structure.
===============================================================================
*/
